\begin{proposition}
    $$ sn(G_{n,3})\geq \left(\frac{1}{3}-o(1)\right)n$$ a.a.s.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
    Let $G_{n,3} = (V,E)$. $G_{n,3}$ contains a Hamiltonian cycle $H$ a.a.s.\ by \cite{robinson1992almost}. Consider for the sake of contradiction that there is a Sudoku set $S$ with $|S| =\left(\frac{1}{3}-\epsilon\right)n$. Our goal is to show for any $\overline{S} = V\setminus S$, $G[\overline{S}]$ contains a cycle a.a.s.

    Lets consider an auxiliary graph $G'=(V',E')$ defined from $G$ and $S$ in the following way. First, index the vertices of $S$ so that $H$ traverses them in order.  For each pair of vertices $v_i,v_{i+1 \mod |S|} \in S$, create a vertex $u_i \in V'$. Add an edge $u_iu_j\in E'$ for each vertex in between $v_i$ and $v_{i+1 \mod |S|}$ in $H$ which is adjacent to a vertex between $v_j$ and $v_{j+1 \mod |S|}$ in $H$.   

    Note that each vertex has degree 3 in $G$, therefore $G\setminus E(H)$ has $\frac{n}{2}$ total edges, and a maximum of $|S| = \left(\frac{1}{3}-\epsilon\right)n$ are incident to $S$. Therefore there are a minimum of $\left(\frac{1}{6}+\epsilon\right)n$ edges of  $G[\overline{S}]\setminus E(H)$. 

    Firstly, Lemma \ref{forest} guarantees that $G[\overline{S}]$ is a forest, meaning $|E(G[\overline{S}])| < |\overline{S}|$. As each vertex in $G$ has degree 3, this implies $|E(\overline{S}, S)| > |\overline{S}|$. Therefore of the  $\binom{n}{\left(\frac{1}{3}-\epsilon\right)n}$ possible sets of vertices, we need only consider those which meet this edge requirement. The probability that a set meets this criteria is at most $\prod_{i=0}^{|S|-1}\frac{3|\overline{S}|-i}{3n-1-2i}$, i.e. the probability of successively selecting $|S|$ edges which all connect to a vertex in $\overline{S}$. Using markov's inequality, we can say that with probability $1-\frac{1}{\ln(n)}$, the number of potential Sudoku sets does not exceed $\ln(n)\cdot\prod_{i=0}^{|S|-1}\frac{3|\overline{S}|-i}{3n-1-2i}\cdot\binom{n}{|S|} \leq \ln(n)\cdot(\frac13)^|S|\cdot\binom{3|\overline{S}|}{|S|}$





\end{proof}

