\subsubsection{Generate Antibody Variants with Strong Affinity to H1N1 Influenza Virus}
\label{sec:case_study}
To assess whether protein models can generate antibody variants with improved binding to a specific antigen, we conducted a case study using F045-092 \cite{ohshima2011naturally}, a naturally occurring antibody that targets the hemagglutinin (HA) protein of influenza H3N2. Notably, F045-092 fails to bind the H1N1 strain California2009 due to a steric barrier at the HA receptor-binding site \cite{ekiert2012cross, simmons2023new}. No experimentally determined structure of the F045-H1N1 complex exists in public databases, and this particular pair has not been studied in prior antibody design literature. As such, our study represent a completely novel setting, free from data leakage. The structure used in our study was computationally predicted using AlphaFold3.
%No known F045-092 variant binds H1N1, making it an ideal target to evaluate generative performance without data leakage.
%We evaluate whether the proteins model can generate antibody variants with strong binding affinity that bind to a specific antigen of interest. As a case study, we chose the naturally-occuring antibody, F045-092 \cite{ohshima2011naturally}, which binds to the haemagglutinin (HA) protein of influenza viruses through a long CDR-H3 loop. While this antibody has strong binding affinity to a wide spectrum of influenza strains in H3N2 \cite{ohshima2011naturally}, it loses its binding to the pandemic H1N1 strain, i.e., California2009., which possesses a single-point mutation at a critical position at the receptor binding sites critical for the binding of F045-092 antibody \cite{simmons2023new}. As of 2025, there is no known F045-092 variants that bind to H1N1, which is ideal for us to evaluate model performance without data leakage.  Accordingly, we used the benchmark models to design F045-092 variants to strengthen the binding affinity to the H1N1 strain. 
We used four representative models—\texttt{ESM-IF}, \texttt{SaProt}, \texttt{DiffAb}, and \texttt{MEAN}—to redesign the CDR-H3 loop of F045-092 while allowing up to five substitution to maintain H3N2 cross-reactivity. Each model generated 1,500 CDR-H3 variants from an input consisting of the masked F045-092 sequence and a predicted complex structure with the H1N1 HA1 protein (Supplement \ref{sup:sec:case_study}). Sampling strategies varied by model, including autoregressive prediction (\texttt{SaProt}), greedy heuristics (\texttt{ESM-IF}), and diffusion-based or graph-based generation (\texttt{DiffAb}, \texttt{MEAN}; see Supplement \ref{sup:sec:sec:design_cdrh3} for details).
