\subsection{Generate Antibody Variants with Strong Affinity to H1N1 Influenza Virus}
We evaluated whether selected models could generate antibody variants with stronger binding affinity to H1N1 than the wild-type F045-092. Each model produced 1,500 CDR-H3 variants with up to five mutations. As \texttt{DiffAb} often generated wild-type sequences with altered structures, we excluded duplicates, resulting in 467 valid \texttt{DiffAb} variants and a total of 4,967 unique variants across all models.
%We investigated whether selected protein models can generate antibody variants with binding affinities stronger than the wild type.  In total, we sampled 1,500 variants per model with mutational depth up to 5. \texttt{DiffAb} generated CDR-H3 sequence that are the same to the wild type with just different complex structures. We filtered out those variants as our goal is to design new antibody variants, which gave 467 out of 1500. So, in total we had 4,967 variants.
In Phase 1 screening, \texttt{ESM-IF} and \texttt{SaProt} produced variants with both strong binding potential and high sequence plausibility. Their average $\Delta \Delta G$ values were -29.27 and -20.79, respectively, indicating substantial improvement in binding energy (Fig. \ref{fig:phase1-screening}, \ref{fig:phase1}A). These variants also retained \texttt{AntiBERTy} plausibility scores (-0.663 for \texttt{ESM-IF} and -0.666 for \texttt{SaProt}) close to that of the wild type (-0.655; Fig. \ref{fig:phase1}B).
%In Phase 1 screening, we found that \texttt{ESM-IF} and \texttt{SaProt} can generate biologically plausible antibody variants with stronger binding affinity than wild type. Specifically, variants generated from \texttt{ESM-IF} and \texttt{SaProt} showed lowest binding energy decrease ($\Delta \Delta G$ of -29.27 and -20.79, respectively, Fig. \ref{fig:phase1-screening}, \ref{fig:phase1}A), while \texttt{ESM-IF} and \texttt{SaProt} achieved similar sequence plausibility (-0.663 and -0.666) to wild type (-0.655) (Fig. \ref{fig:phase1-screening}, \ref{fig:phase1}B).
On the other hands, \texttt{DiffAb} generated plausible sequences (-0.657) but failed to improve binding energy ($\Delta \Delta G = 2.03$), suggesting mutations without affinity gain. In contrast, \texttt{MEAN} produced variants with improved binding ($\Delta \Delta G = -13.95$), but at the cost of lower sequence plausibility (-0.680), indicating a trade-off between biophysical fitness and evolutionary realism (Fig. \ref{fig:phase1}).
%On the other hands, \texttt{DiffAb} generated antibody variants that are biologically plausible (sequence plausibility of -0.657), but its binding energy change $\Delta \Delta G$ was positive ($2.03$), implying no improvement in binding compared to wild type. \texttt{MEAN}-generated sequences are less biologically plausible (sequence plausibility of -0.680) than wild type, but they achieved stronger binding affinity than wild type ($\Delta \Delta G =-13.95$, Fig. \ref{fig:phase1}). 
