\section{Conclusion and Limitations}
\label{conclustions}
%predicting  a given antibody's bindinng affinity was relatiely easier than actually designing CDR-H3 with strong binding affinity
This study introduces AbBiBench, a biologically relevant and structurally informed benchmarking framework for antibody binding affinity maturation and optimization.  Recognizing the limitations of traditional computational evaluation metrics, our approach explicitly incorporates antibody-antigen complex information, thereby aligning more closely with the biological realities of antibody interactions.  
Our results demonstrate that global structure-informed protein language model used for inverse folding methods, such as \texttt{ESM-IF} and \texttt{ProteinMPNN}, outperform other evaluated computational models, primarily due to their effective integration of structural context. 
%Our results demonstrate that inverse folding methods, such as \texttt{ESM-IF} and \texttt{ProteinMPNN}, outperform other evaluated computational models, primarily due to their effective integration of structural context.
%In contrast, standard biophysical metrics, including \texttt{FoldX} energy calculations and epitope surface area, showed limited predictive capability when used in isolation.
In a case study focused on redesigning the F045-092 antibody for binding to the H1N1 influenza subtype, we identified 18 Pareto-optimal antibody variants with improved predicted affinity and structural integrity. These 18 variants have been successfully expressed in vitro, and wet lab experiments to quantify their binding affinity to H1N1 hemagglutinin are currently underway. The results of these assays will directly validate the computational predictions and help assess the true binding potential of the designed antibodies.
Limitations of this study include the lack of currently available experimental neutralization data and the relatively small size of some benchmark datasets (Supplement \ref{sup:sec:limitations}). In future work, we plan to expand the benchmark to include additional therapeutic properties such as stability, immunogenicity, and developability, and to incorporate functional assay data (e.g., IC50) to further align model evaluations with biological outcomes. 
%AbBiBench serves as a robust foundation for advancing function-aware antibody design and supporting the development of next-generation therapeutic antibodies.


%A limitation of this study is that experimental binding and neutralization assays are still ongoing. and the small size of certain benchmark datasets; further discussion is provided in Supplement Section~\ref{sup:sec:limitations}. Future work will extend beyond affinity, incorporating multi-objective evaluation—including stability, immunogenicity, and developability—to better reflect therapeutic design constraints. We also plan to integrate functional assay data (e.g., IC50) to ground model evaluation in real-world biological outcomes. Ultimately, our benchmark provides an essential foundation for rigorous, biologically-informed evaluation of antibody design models, facilitating progress toward more effective and clinically impactful antibody therapeutics. 
%we validated a two-stage computational pipeline—combining rapid initial screening with detailed structural refinement—to successfully identify antibody variants exhibiting significantly improved predicted binding affinities. 
%Our benchmark framework underscores the crucial role of structure-informed metrics, such as AlphaFold3-generated models and inverse folding likelihoods, in effectively guiding antibody sequence design.  


%Our study has two main limitations. 
%\sout{Our study has the following limitations. First, the absence of experimental neutralization readouts (e.g., IC\(_{50}\)) restricts the ability of generative models to design therapeutic antibodies with tighter binding and stronger potency. Therefore, future studies should incorporate complementary functional assay data, providing more biologically relevant training signals for generative models and enhancing the reliability of their predictions. Second, our reliance on purely computational metrics to estimate N-fold binding may not fully capture real-world binding behavior. In-depth structural analyses and direct experimental validation remain essential for confirming actual receptor-antibody interactions and validating computational predictions. \textcolor{red}{Third, while our benchmark includes diverse antigens and nearly 150K antibody variants, some datasets (e.g., 1mhp, 1n8z) are limited in size or derived from narrow mutational libraries, potentially impacting the statistical power of per-dataset evaluations.}

%In the future, we aim to expand experimental datasets, refine computational methods to integrate additional antibody characteristics, and enhance the synergy between computational prediction and experimental validation. Ultimately, our benchmark provides an essential foundation for rigorous, biologically-informed evaluation of antibody design models, facilitating progress toward more effective and clinically impactful antibody therapeutics.}


