\paragraph{Background and Notation.}
We denote vectors by lowercase bold letters (e.g., $\x$) and matrices by uppercase bold letters (e.g., $\X$). 
%For any $n \in \mathbb{N}$, w
We write $[n]$ to denote the index set $\{1, \dots, n\}$. The unit $\ell_2$-ball in $\mathbb{R}^d$ is defined as $\mathcal{B} = \{\x \in \mathbb{R}^d \mid \|\x\|_2 \leq 1\}$. 
%Elementwise non-negativity of vectors or matrices is denoted by $\x \geq \bm{0}$ and $\X \geq \bm{0}$, respectively.
%
We consider a general multimodal representation learning setting in which a vision-language model maps image or text inputs $\x \in \mathcal{X}$ into a shared representation space $\mathcal{A} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$. 
A collection of $n$ such embeddings is stored in a matrix $\A \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d}$.
%, where each row $\A_i \in \mathbb{R}^d$ corresponds to a sample $i \in [n]$.
Dictionary Learning seeks to approximate $\A$ as a linear combination of concept vectors from a dictionary $\D \in \mathbb{R}^{c \times d}$, using a sparse code matrix $\Z \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times c}$:
%\vspace{-3.5mm}
\begin{align}
(\Z^\star, \D^\star) = \arg \min_{\Z, \D} \|\A - \Z\D\|_F^2
\end{align}
%\vspace{-3.5mm}
where $\|\cdot\|_F$ denotes the Frobenius norm, and dictionary atoms are constrained to lie on $\mathcal{B}$.
%Different assumptions on $\Z$ and $\D$ yield known formulations: if each $\Z_i$ is restricted to a one-hot vector in $\{\bm{e}_1, \dots, \bm{e}_c\}$, the problem reduces to $k$-Means, where $\D$ contains cluster centroids. If both $\Z \geq \bm{0}$ and $\D \geq \bm{0}$, the setting corresponds to Non-negative Matrix Factorization (NMF). 
%Prior work~\cite{zhang2021invertible,fel2023craft} has demonstrated that NMF often yields highly interpretable concept bases according to controlled human experiments. 
%However, the activations of modern vision-language models are not constrained to be non-negative. This motivates the use of Semi-NMF~\cite{bhalla2025interpreting,parekh2025concept}, where the sparse codes are constrained to be non-negative ($\Z \geq \bm{0}$), but the dictionary $\D$ is left unconstrained.


\paragraph{Sparse Autoencoders.}
In the case of Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs), an encoder network $\encoder$, a single-layer MLP, maps embeddings $\A$ to sparse codes $\Z$ via a linear transformation and a projection  \citep{hindupur2025projecting} operator $\projection{\cdot}$:
\begin{align}
\Z = \encoder(\A) = \projection{\A \W + \bm{b}}
\end{align}
where $\W \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times c}$ and $\bm{b} \in \mathbb{R}^c$ are learnable encoder parameters. In particular, we consider several SAEs that can be described by their projection operators such as:
\begin{align}
\projection{\x} =
\begin{cases}
\mathrm{ReLU}(\x) = \max(\bm{0}, \x) \\[0.3em]
\mathrm{JumpReLU}(\x) = \max(\bm{0}, \x - \bm{\theta}) + \bm{\theta} \odot H(\x - \bm{\theta}) \\[0.3em]
\mathrm{TopK}(\x, k) = \arg \min_{\z \in \mathbb{R}^c} \|\z - \x\|_2^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \|\z\|_0 = k, \; \z \geq \bm{0}
\end{cases}
\end{align}

Where $H$ is the Heaviside step function. We will also explore a simple extension, \textit{BatchTopK}, which flexibly shares applies the TopK operation across the entire batch. 
%This allows for flexible control of sparsity while maintaining compatibility with mini-batch training.
%
\paragraph{Expressivity vs Sparsity.}
%
An ideal dictionary learning method should produce representations that are both \textbf{expressive} and \textbf{sparse}. In practice, this means that model embeddings should activate only a small number of meaningful concepts, indicating that the learned dictionary is both specific and semantically aligned with the data. We apply five dictionary learning methods to four models (CLIP, SigLIP and SigLIP2, which are encoder models, and AIMv2 which is an autregressive vision-language model). The activation matrix $\A$ consists of 600,000 normalized embeddings from passing the COCO dataset \citep{coco2014} through the models, with each $\A_i \in \mathcal{B}$, the unit $\ell_2$-ball.
