\paragraph{Dictionary learning methods}
To understand the linear features of the models, we train multiple different dictionary learning methods on all four models: Semi-Nonnegative Matrix Factorization (Semi-NMF)~\citep{parekh2025concept}, Sparse Autoencoders (SAEs), Top-K SAEs~\citep{gao2024scaling}, JumpReLU SAEs~\citep{rajamanoharan2024jumping}, and BatchTopK SAEs~\citep{bussmann2024batchtopk} \thomas{citations, sorry!}. For a set of $n$ model embeddings $A \in R^{n \times d}$, the main idea behind these methods is that they express these embeddings as linear combination of concept vectors from a dictionary $D$ with coefficients (or, codes) $Z$. Though they vary in their auxiliary optimization constraints, the main objective of all the dictionary learning methods is to find $Z$ and $D$ such that the embeddings in $A$ are optimally reconstructed:
\begin{align}
    (\bm{Z}^*, \bm{D}^*) &= \arg \min_{\bm{Z}, \bm{D}} \|\bm{A} - \bm{Z}\bm{D}\|^2_{F} 
\end{align}
Where \(\| \cdot \|_F\) is the Frobenius norm. This optimization can be done in different ways. In the case of SAEs,
\isabel{finish} 
We provide more thorough details about hyperparameters and SAE training in \Cref{sec:app-hyperparameters} \thomas{Add hyperparameters}

Note that $D$ defines the \textit{algebraic structure} of the linear features: for any new activations $A'$ that we throw at the dictionary, even very different ones, they will be reconstructed using the same concepts from $D$. $Z$, on the other hand, reflects the \textit{dataset statistics}: different embeddings have to use different $Z$ values in order to pick out the best concepts to be reconstructed with. This is an important distinction to keep in mind as we use these linear features to understand the internals of vision language models. For a good dictionary learning method, $D$ tells us the overall concept directions important to the model, while $Z$ can tell us how these concepts are used to relate image and text inputs and contribute to the functioning of the model.  

We want dictionary learning methods to be \textbf{expressive yet sparse}. Ideally, the embeddings of the model should be activated using just a few concepts from $D$, meaning that the concepts are expressive and specific. To compare the dictionary learning methods that we trained on the four models, we compare their tradeoff between different levels of sparsity (where we change $\|Z\|_0$, the number of non-zero entries in $Z$) and reconstruction quality, which we measure using $R^2$, the sum of the squared differences between ground-truth and reconstructed embeddings in our test set \thomas{This is what the y-axis is right?}. Our results are in \Cref{fig:pareto}, indicating that BatchTopK SAEs fare the best along this Pareto Frontier for all models. In subsequent experiments, we will run our analyses on our BatchTopK SAEs for all models. 

