\subsection{Metrics: The Geometry and Statistics of Learned Concepts}
%\vspace{-2mm}

To assess the interpretability and quality of the learned representations, we introduce four complementary metrics that go beyond the standard sparsity–reconstruction trade-off: \textbf{stability}, \textbf{energy}, \textbf{modality score}, and \textbf{bridge score}. Each of these measures captures a different axis of representational information to better understand the representation of VLM using the SAEs.

\paragraph{Stability.} Stability quantifies the consistency of learned dictionaries under different training runs with random initialization. If two independently trained SAEs on the same model produce significantly different dictionaries, it suggests that the learned concepts are not robust, which weakens their interpretability. Following \citet{fel2025archetypal} and \citet{spielman2012exact}, we compute the stability between two dictionaries $\D, \D' \in \mathbb{R}^{c \times d}$ by optimally aligning their rows using the Hungarian algorithm, which solves for a permutation matrix $\permut \in \mathcal{P}(c)$ that maximizes the total similarity between matched concept vectors:
%\vspace{-2mm}
\begin{align}
    \text{Stability}(\bm{D}, \bm{D}') = \max_{\permut \in \mathcal{P}(c)} \frac{1}{c} \operatorname{Tr}(\bm{D}^\top \permut \bm{D}').
    \label{eq:stability}
\end{align}
%\vspace{-2mm}

A higher stability score indicates that concept representations are reproducible across training runs, reinforcing their semantic reliability.

\paragraph{Energy.}  
While stability measures whether concepts are consistently learned, it does not tell us \emph{which} concepts matter most in practice. To better understand which concepts are actually \textit{used} by the model, we turn to the energy of each concept. Energy captures the average activation strength of a concept across a dataset, revealing which parts of the dictionary are consistently involved in reconstructions.
Formally, for concept $i$, we define its energy as: $\text{Energy}_i = \mathbb{E}_{\z}(z_i)$. 
%Since the dictionary vectors $\D_i$ are normalized to lie in the unit $\ell_2$ ball (i.e., $\D_i \in \mathcal{B}$), most of the ``power'' in the reconstruction $\A \approx \Z\D$ is carried by the activations in $\Z$. The energy thus tells us which concepts are frequently active, helping us distinguish between influential concepts and rarely used ones. 
Energy provides a \emph{statistical} view over the dataset, revealing where attention should be focused for interpreting the learned representation.

\paragraph{Modality Score.}  
In the context of VLMs, a central question is whether a concept is modality-specific or shared across modalities. To probe this, we introduce the modality score, which quantifies how much a concept contributes to reconstructing image versus text inputs.
Let $\iota$ and $\tau$ be the empirical distributions of sparse codes $\z$ from image ($\iota$) and text ($\tau$) inputs, respectively. We define the modality score of a concept $i$ as the fraction of expected activation energy assigned to image-based inputs:
\begin{align}
    \text{ModalityScore}_i = 
    \frac{\mathbb{E}_{\z \sim \iota}(z_i)}
    {\mathbb{E}_{\z \sim \iota}(z_i) + \mathbb{E}_{\z \sim \tau}(z_i)}.
    \label{eq:modality}
\end{align}
%Here, $z_i$ denotes the average value for the concept $i$. 
A modality score near $1$ indicates that the concept fires only image inputs, while a score near $0$ suggests text dominance. A value around $\frac{1}{2}$ indicates a multi-modal concept.

\paragraph{Bridge Score.}  
While the modality score offers a per-concept perspective, many vision–language tasks rely on coordinated interactions between concepts across modalities. 
%Crucially, alignment between images and text is often achieved not through isolated concepts, but through pairs of concepts that co-activate and remain semantically aligned. 
To capture these interactions, we introduce the \textbf{bridge matrix} $\mathbf{B} \in \mathbb{R}^{c \times c}$, which quantifies how pairs of concepts contribute to the alignment of modalities.
Let $(\z_\iota, \z_\tau) \sim \gamma$ be a pair of sparse codes obtained from a matching image–text input pair, drawn from a joint empirical distribution $\gamma$ over aligned data points. Let $\D \in \mathbb{R}^{c \times d}$ denote the shared dictionary. The bridge matrix is defined as:
%\vspace{-2mm}
\begin{align}
    \label{eq:bridge}
    \mathbf{B} = 
    \underbrace{\mathbb{E}_{(\z_\iota, \z_\tau) \sim \gamma} 
    (\z_\iota^\top \z_\tau)}_{\text{co-activation}} 
    \odot 
    \underbrace{(\D \D^\top),}_{\text{alignment}}
\end{align}
%\vspace{-2mm}

with $\odot$ being the Hadamard product. The first term captures statistical co-activation between concepts: how frequently concept $i$ in the image code and concept $j$ in the text code are simultaneously active for semantically aligned inputs. The second term reflects the geometric alignment between the corresponding dictionary atoms, computed as the cosine similarity between dictionary atoms
%$\D_i$ and $\D_j$ (since $\D_i \in \mathcal{B}$, the unit $\ell_2$ ball). 
The elementwise product combines both aspects—activation and alignment—into a single interpretable structure.

The resulting matrix $\mathbf{B}$ reveals how concepts jointly operate across modalities. A large value at position $\mathbf{B}_{i,j}$ indicates that concepts $i$ and $j$ frequently co-activate and encode similar directions in the shared embedding space. These concept pairs act as semantic bridges, facilitating the alignment of image and text embeddings.

\textbf{In this sense, the bridge matrix provides insight into why the model succeeds at cross-modal alignment}. It highlights not just which concepts are shared, but how they collaborate structurally and statistically to support the model's semantic integration of vision and language.

