\subsection{Beyond the usual trade-off: Stability, Energy, Modality Score, and Bridge Score}
\label{sec:definitions-stability}

In order to analyze and evaluate the concepts our SAEs arrive at, we will rely on four quantities that we define here: stability, energy, modality score, and bridge score.  

\textbf{Stability is a quantity that relates how similar two SAEs are}, and we use it to calculate the effect of random variation \citep{fel2025archetypal}: if the dictionary of an SAE is very different from the dictionary of another SAE trained on the same model, then this would indicate that the SAEs are not consistently extracting the same concepts, making them less meaningful for interpretability. Following \citet{fel2025archetypal} and \citet{spielman2012exact}, we measure stability using the Hungarian Algorithm, finding an optimal permutation matrix $\Pi$ from the set of all permutations $\mathcal{P}(n)$ that matches the rows of the two dictionary matrices $D$ and $D'$, maximizing the dot product between matched rows.  

\begin{align}
    \text{Stability}(\bm{D}, \bm{D}') = \max_{\bm{\Pi} \in \mathcal{P}(n)} \frac{1}{n} \text{Tr}(\bm{D}^\top \bm{\Pi}\bm{D}')
\end{align}

In order to get a more complete picture of how SAE dictionaries change between different runs, we also need a measure of importance: how much each concept in the dictionary is actually contributing to the reconstruction of the activations. If a concept is present in one dictionary but never used (or used extremely rarely), perhaps it is not as important that it be captured in all other dictionaries under random variation. \textbf{To measure the contribution of each concept, we calculate the energy value}: the expectation of the $Z$ values, or coefficients, that are assigned to the concept. For every concept $i$, the energy is the expectation of the coefficient $z_i$ assigned to concept $i$ for every activation $a$ in a test set of activations $A$:

\begin{align}
    \text{Energy}(i) = \mathbb{E}_a(z_i)
    \label{eq:energy}
\end{align}

This measures how much each concept contributes to reconstructing the original activations. 

Another energy metric that we want to know for each concept in the vision-language model space is its \textbf{modality score: how much it contributes to reconstructing image activations versus text activations.} The activations $a$ that we use to get our energy estimation are balanced between the two modalities, coming from two equal-sized matrices $A_\text{text}$ and $A_\text{image}$. To measure a concept's contribution to reconstructing text and image activations separately, we define the modality score as the percentage of the $z$ coefficients which are used in reconstructing image activations:

