Our first observation is that the majority of concepts activate predominantly on a single modality—either text or image. As defined in \Cref{eq:modality}, the modality score of a concept measures the fraction of its total activation energy that comes from image inputs. A score near 1 indicates image-specific usage; a score near 0 indicates text-specific usage.
%
In \Cref{fig:modality-score-histograms}, we plot the modality score distribution across all concepts for SAEs trained on each model. The top row shows raw counts: while some concepts cluster around 0.5, the distributions are clearly bimodal, with dominant modes at 0 and 1. This pattern becomes more pronounced when we weight each concept by its energy (right): almost all of the energy across all models is concentrated on concepts that are nearly exclusive to one modality. 

We conclude \textbf{that the most influential directions in the SAE dictionary --- those responsible for the bulk of reconstruction --- are overwhelmingly single-modality}. Despite the shared embedding space, the sparse structure learned by the SAE reveals a strong separation between text and image representations.

\paragraph{Cross-Modal Alignment from Unimodal Bases}
Interestingly, the predominance of single-modality concepts in the SAE dictionary does not preclude the model's ability to align across modalities. This sets up a central \textit{tension}: if the concepts that dominate reconstruction are modality-specific, how does the model support semantic connections between paired image–text inputs?
%
The resolution lies in the structure of the SAE dictionary itself. While most concepts activate primarily on one modality, they do not exist in isolation. The sparse autoencoder enables \textit{bridging} via geometrically aligned and co-activated concept pairs—concepts that live in distinct modality clusters but consistently respond to semantically related inputs.

\begin{InsightBox}
\textbf{Finding}: Despite being functionally unimodal, concepts can form semantically meaningful cross-modal bridges through high co-activation and directional alignment. These bridges support joint understanding across text and image even without shared concepts.
\end{InsightBox}

As illustrated in \Cref{fig:bridge-viz}, high-bridge-score connections often capture many-to-many relationships—for example, a visual concept like ``surfboards'' aligns not just to a single caption, but to a \textit{diverse cluster of related descriptions}. This illustrates how the model achieves flexible semantic grounding across modalities using sparse but structured interconnections.

