\paragraph{How can concepts be both single-modality and almost orthogonal to modality? The SAE projection effect.}

The results reveal an apparent contradiction: many concepts activate almost exclusively for one modality (\Cref{fig:modality-score-histograms}), yet their directions are poor modality classifiers (\Cref{fig:classifier-histograms}), suggesting they lie near the modality-agnostic subspace. How can a concept be geometrically neutral yet functionally single-modality?

The resolution lies in the SAE projection step: activations are encoded as $\Z = \projection{\A \W + \bm{b}}$, where $\projection{\cdot}$ enforces sparsity (e.g., TopK or ReLU). Even if a concept direction is nearly orthogonal to modality, one modality may consistently produce higher pre-activation scores, causing it to be selected more often. This leads to modality-specific usage driven by \textbf{modality-skewed thresholding}, rather than geometry. We illustrate this effect in \Cref{fig:projection}.



