\subsubsection{SC Decoding Information Encoded on Multiple Input Strands}
\par We now extend the previous single-strand experiment to the case where information is encoded across multiple DNA strands, following the approach in Section~\ref{sec:experiments_synthetic}. This design mitigates variability in trace numbers per strand.

\par Figure~\ref{fig:dna-nanopore-3} shows the code rate necessary to achieve a \gls{ber} of approximately $0.01$ and compares it to the estimated average \gls{mi} per bit as a function of the number of concatenated strands $N_0$. We stack up to $2048$ strands, yielding block lengths up to $2^{19}$ bits. 

\par Since the test set contains only $7500$ sequences, we use the following sampling method: for each sample, we draw $N_0$ strands, concatenate their binary representations, and generate a codeword of length $256N_0$ bits. For each concatenated set, the number of traces per strand is sampled as $k \sim \operatorname{Poiss}(5)$, and embeddings are computed independently per trace. The numerical results confirm that the observations made on synthetic DNA data generalize well to real Nanopore data.

\subsubsection{Performance under Realistic Sequencing Budget Constraints}
