\begin{abstract}
    %Spiking neural networks (SNNs) have emerged as a groundbreaking innovation in neural networks, primarily due to their superior energy efficiency and alignment with biological processes. Despite significant progress in SNN implementations, most existing models continue to mimic the architecture of conventional artificial neural networks, hampering their ability to replicate the dynamic and efficient interactions observed in biological neurons. To overcome this limitation, we explore the potential of a random graph architecture (RGA) based paradigm of SNN, which is named Cognition-aware Spiking Neural Network (CogniSNN). In addition to validate the feasibility of RGA-based SNN, we assign both \textbf{expandability} and \textbf{neuroplasticity} to this model. More precisely, we present an enhanced spiking residual learning technique designed to combat network degradation within deeper graph pathways, thereby supporting efficient and scalable learning for large-scale tasks. We then incorporate a critical path-based learning algorithm that endows the CogniSNN with the inherent capacity of SNNs for continual learning, by utilizing both data features and graph structures. Our experimental findings reveal that CogniSNN attains cutting-edge performance on neuromorphic datasets with less parameters and timesteps. Remarkably, CogniSNN achieves an accuracy of $95.5\%$ on the DVS-Gesture dataset with just 5 timesteps. Furthermore, the critical path-based learning approach decreases the forgetting rate by $3\% \sim 5\%$ by keep performance as well as other continual learning based SNNs. This study marks that the integration of RGA with SNNs proposes an innovative paradigm for biologically inspired neural networks. The code is accessible at \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/CogniSNN-B1CC}.

    %Spiking neural network (SNN) has emerged as a groundbreaking innovation in neural networks, primarily due to their superior energy efficiency and alignment with biological processes. However, the architecture of existing SNN models still imitates traditional artificial neural networks (ANNs), restricting their ability to fully emulate dynamics of biological neuron interaction. 

    Despite advances in spiking neural networks (SNNs) in numerous tasks, their architectures remain highly similar to traditional artificial neural networks (ANNs), restricting their ability to mimic natural connections between biological neurons. This paper develops a new modeling paradigm for SNN with random graph architecture (RGA), termed Cognition-aware SNN (CogniSNN). Furthermore, we improve the \textbf{expandability} and \textbf{neuroplasticity} of CogniSNN by introducing a modified spiking residual neural node (ResNode) to counteract network degradation in deeper graph pathways, as well as a critical path-based algorithm that enables CogniSNN to perform continual learning on new tasks leveraging the features of the data and the RGA learned in the old task. Experiments show that CogniSNN with re-designed ResNode performs outstandingly in neuromorphic datasets with fewer parameters, achieving $95.5\%$ precision in the DVS-Gesture dataset with only 5 timesteps. The critical path-based approach decreases $3\%$ to $5\%$ forgetting while maintaining expected performance in learning new tasks that are similar to or distinct from the old ones. This study showcases the potential of RGA-based SNN and paves a new path for biologically inspired networks based on graph theory. 
    % The code is available at \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/CogniSNN-B1CC}.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Spiking neural networks (SNNs), inspired by biological nervous systems, provide enhanced biological interpretability and energy efficiency over traditional artificial neural networks (ANNs). %Using event-driven computation and complex spatiotemporal dynamics, SNNs are adept at real-time, low-power tasks on portable devices, which has attracted significant interest. 
Recently, various prominent SNN models have been developed, such as Spike VGG ~\cite{2021_rathi_diet}, Spike ResNet ~\cite{2021_fang_deep}, and Spike Transformer ~\cite{2022_zhou_spikformer}. These models maintain the performance benefits of conventional ANNs while leveraging the intrinsic energy efficiency of SNNs, achieving improved accuracy on neuromorphic datasets. 

Despite substantial advancements achieved by contemporary SNNs, their fundamental architecture continues to parallel the computational graph framework of ANNs ~\cite{2017_xie_aggregated,2019_xie_exploring}. This framework diverges considerably from the brain's biological configuration, which resembles a random graph architecture (RGA). As early as the 1940s, ~\cite{2004_turing_intelligent} likened randomly connected unorganized machines to an infant's cerebral cortex, establishing the link between computational models and neuroscience. ~\cite{1958_rosenblatt_perceptron} later suggested that the setup of each organism's nervous system is primarily random and distinct at birth. The research by ~\cite{2011_varshney_structural,1998_watts_collective} showed that the nematode nervous system is a random, small-world graph with about 300 neurons, which further supports the RGA of the biological system. Besides, it is crucial to recognize that if SNN still inherits the technological framework of conventional ANN, it may encounter similar issues related to hyperparameter configuration in ANNs, such as the number of layers, leading to performance barriers. These insights urge a re-evaluation of the SNN framework through the RGA perspective.

In contrast, employing RGA in the architecture design of SNNs could be a potential solution to the aforementioned bottleneck by using graph theory to account for the spatial features of neural network topology. In practice, a similar effort was made by Xie et al. who performed experiments to validate that RGA-based ANNs achieved equivalent performance to computational graph-based ANN in multiple datasets ~\cite{2019_xie_exploring}. To our knowledge, the most related evidence comes from the investigation conducted by Yan et al. ~\cite{2024_yan_sampling} who performed a neural architecture search on SNN models, indirectly highlighting the promise of RGA-based SNNs. Despite the lack of direct studies on the feasibility of RGA-based SNNs, such intuition can be derived by comparing the phase of spike processing over all neurons in an SNN to graph signal processing, which is highly dependent on the graph structure. 

