\subsubsection{Tailored Pooling Mechanism}
\label{subsubsec: TP}

%The feature misalignment occurs when downsampling intermediate features at the pooling layer stacked after the Resnode. As shown in the 

% downsampling intermediate features is essential~\cite{2018_zoph_learning,2021_kim_optimizing}. For this, we need to introduce the pooling layer after each ResNode, but with that comes the problem that the difficulty of information integration in a downstream ResNode $v_j$ depends on the misalignment of the information (feature) sent from its upstream nodes. To solve this issue, a tailored average pooling strategy is required to regulate high-dimensional features, ensuring that all features are consistently aligned to a uniform dimension.

%Specifically, we use $D(\cdot)$ to represent the spatial dimension of a feature map. 
Given a node $v_j \in \mathcal{V}$ (not the root node), we estimate $D(I_j[t])$ according to the outputs from its predecessors $\mathcal{P}_j$ as follows.

\begin{align}
    D(I_j[t]) = \min \left \{ D(O_{i}[t])\mid i \in \mathcal{P}_j \right \}. 
\end{align}%

\noindent Consequently, $TP(\cdot)$ is defined as follows:

\begin{align}
    TP(O_{i}[t]) = AvgP(O_{i}[t],\frac{D(O_{i}[t])}{D(I_j[t])} ),
    \label{eq: TP}
\end{align}%

\noindent with the significance of implementing average pooling of $O_{i}[t]$ based on the kernel size $\frac{D(O_{i}[t])}{D(I_j[t])}$.


\iffalse
