%The structure of ResNode and the inherent graph attributes of CogniSNN prompt a reconsideration of how SNNs acquire and retain knowledge, which relies not only on computational features across connections but also on the RGA. Unlike other SNN models, ResNode seeks to maintain data predominantly as spikes with either 0 or 1. Consequently, its performance may not match that of real-valued methods, which handle more complex information. However, the experimental results in Section \ref{subsec: feasibility} demonstrate the superior performance of our method. This suggests that the RGA of CogniSNN significantly contributes to knowledge acquisition and retention. In particular, the visualization in Supplementary Material illustrates that specific ResNodes and certain paths (defined as sequences of nodes in CogniSNN with information flow) are activated more frequently than others.

%Based on the above analysis, we propose leveraging certain substructures of CogniSNN, reflecting the brain's neuroplasticity, which involves neural reuse to reshape functions in response to new experiences. To this end, we aim to develop a novel method that diverges from conventional continual or transfer learning techniques, which do not provide a rationale for freezing modules in pre-trained models during adaptation to new tasks. Our study focuses on parameter fine-tuning associated with the selective substructures, termed candidate critical paths, which reflect neural connections in the brain essential for extracting high-level generalized data features. In particular, we give the definition of critical paths as follows.

To capture the neuroplasticity of CogniSNN as elucidated in the Introduction, we focus on parameter fine-tuning associated with the selective substructures, termed critical paths to achieve the neural reuse to reshape functions in response to new experiences. This idea is inspired by visualizing the activation frequency of nodes. As shown in \textbf{Figure 11} of Supplementary Material, it can be observed that specific ResNodes and certain paths (defined as sequences of nodes in CogniSNN with information flow) are activated more/less frequently than others. Based on such observation, a critical path is targeted based on the concept of betweenness centrality from graph theory which is usually used to quantify the importance of a node (or edge) based on its role in connecting other nodes. In this paper, given a path $p$ formulated as:

\begin{align}
    p = \{ v_{1}, e_{1},v_{2},e_{2},...,e_{l_p},v_{l_p+1} \},
\end{align}%

\noindent where $l_p$ is length of $p$, indicating the number of edges involved in $p$, we define the betweenness centrality of $p$, denoted by $C_B(p)$ as follows.

\begin{definition}
    $C_B(p) = \sum_{i=1}^{l_p+1} C_{B}(v_{i}) + \sum_{j=1}^{l_p} C_{B}(e_{j}).$
    \label{eq: C_B(p)}
\end{definition}


%To evaluate the structural superiority of CogniSNN, we test the model using continual learning scenarios similar to human brain learning. 

% We use the LWF method as the foundational approach for continual learning. Specifically, we only train the parameters of one path and optimize the loss function.


In Definition \ref{eq: C_B(p)}, $C_{B}(v_{i})$ and $C_{B}(e_{j})$ are the betweenness centrality of a node and an edge, which follows the principle of graph theory \footnote{The definition of node betweenness centrality and edge betweenness centrality are in \textbf{Section 4} of Supplementart Material.}. Based on the definition of $C_B(p)$, we sort all paths in descending order according to their betweenness centrality:

\begin{align}
    P = \{ p_{1},p_{2},...,p_{|P|} \},
\end{align}%

\noindent where $C_{B}(p_{1})\ge C_{B}(p_{2}) ...\ge C_{B}(p_{|P|})$, $|P|$ is the total number of paths. We then define the critical paths ($\mathcal{C}$) as a set of paths with capacity $K$ according to the definition of $C_B(p)$ and the concept related to the similarity between old and new tasks, mathematically formulated as follows.

\begin{definition}
    $\mathcal{C} \triangleq \{C_{B}(p_{k})| k \in [1, K], K \leq |P| \}$ if two tasks are similar. Otherwise, $\mathcal{C} \triangleq \{ C_{B}(p_{k})| k \in [|P|- K + 1, |P|], K \leq |P| \}.$
    \label{def: critical paths}
\end{definition}

To measure the similarity between the datasets, we use the Fréchet Inception Distance (FID)~\cite{2017_heusel_gans}, which is originally designed to evaluate generative models by comparing the differences in the distribution of the generated and real images in the feature space. Based on empirical observations, we consider two datasets similar when their FID is below the threshold of 50. For example, CIFAR100 and CIFAR10 show strong similarity with an FID of \textbf{24.5}, while CIFAR100 and MNIST, having an FID of \textbf{2341.2}, show weak similarity. This definition aligns with human cognitive processes, where principal neural pathways are leveraged for similar tasks, while unused connections are activated for unfamiliar tasks to assimilate new information and avoid forgetting prior knowledge. For simplicity, we set $K = 1$ in this study.

% According to the aforementioned assumption, consider two similar datasets, such as CIFAR100 and CIFAR10. We identify critical paths as those frequently activated in CogniSNN trained based on the source dataset, which are then utilized for retraining on the target dataset. In contrast, for dissimilar datasets like CIFAR100 and MNIST, critical paths are rarely activated. This assumption is justified because it performs similar to human learning capabilities. The high-level features we learn in our brain are advantageous for reuse in similar tasks, whereas for novel tasks, underutilized or unused neuron connections would be used to learn new knowledge and in the meanwhile prevent catastrophic forgetting.


