Consequently, we analyze that the high betweenness centrality paths in CogniSNN tend to learn common features in the dataset. Therefore, during continual learning with similar datasets, the high betweenness centrality paths need to be fine-tuned to better adapt to the new task. In contrast, the low betweenness centrality path tends to learn irrelevant features in the dataset. Therefore, when encountering datasets with very different distributions, it is necessary to fine-tune the low betweenness centrality path to learn new features for better adaptation to the new task.
