To see that $\C$ fulfills this property, we use the following Theorem:
\begin{theorem}
\label{thm:ts}
Let $n \geq 2$. For any workflow nets $M_1, \dots, M_n \in \mathcal{M}$ we get:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\C(\seqop(M_1, \dots, M_n)) = \C(M_1) + \dots + \C(M_n)$,
\item $\C(\parop(M_1, \dots, M_n)) = \C(M_1) + \dots + \C(M_n) + n-1$,
\item $\C(\choiceop(M_1, \dots, M_n)) = \C(M_1) + \dots + \C(M_n)$ and
\item $\C(\loopop(M_1, \dots, M_n)) = \C(M_1) + \dots + \C(M_n)$.
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The claim is obivous for the operations $\seqop$, $\choiceop$ and $\loopop$, since none of the out-going arrows of transitions in $M_1, \dots, M_n$ are changed and no new transition with more than one out-going arc is introduced by these operations.
For $\parop$, however, we introduce the new transition $t_i^*$ that has $n$ out-going arcs. Therefore, the token split complexity increases by $n-1$ in this case. \hfill $\square$
\end{proof}
Thus, according to Theorem~\ref{thm:ts}, $\C(\oplus(M_1, \dots, M_n)) \geq \C(M_i)$ for any workflow nets $M_1, \dots, M_n \in \mathcal{M}$, any operation $\oplus \in \{\seqop, \parop, \choiceop, \loopop\}$ and a model $M_i \in \{M_1, \dots, M_n\}$.

