% \autoref{fig:exp3} presents the end-to-end performance of \sys, compared to RR+FCFS and WB+FCFS under \underline{Homo} deployment settings. Across all experimental settings, \sys (WB+PQ) consistently achieves full SLO attainment at significantly lower SLO scales than both baselines. This trend is most pronounced in Trace 3, where the baseline systems require considerably larger SLO scales to achieve 95-100\% attainment. For instance, in the scenario of Trace 3 at 1.0 query per second, WB+PQ attains full completion at a scale of 5, whereas WB+FCFS and RR+FCFS do not reach full attainment until scales of approximately 7 and 8, respectively. In this case, \sys boost the performance by 60\% from the baseline. On the other hand, among all three traces, throughput metrics show that \sys is 1.42-1.49 times higher than the RR+FCFS baseline. These results indicate that \sys not only supports tighter latency constraints but also maintains superior robustness under increasing request loads. The advantage is attributable to the urgency-guided priority queue, which allows more critical requests to be prioritized appropriately, thereby optimizing the system’s responsiveness even in high-throughput regimes.

